{"name":"Douglas Fir Green","hex":"#6f9881","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"douglas","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A habitational surname from Scottish Gaelic of Scottish origin.","A male given name transferred from the surname."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/douglas"},{"term":"fir","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Firan."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A conifer of the genus Abies.","Any pinaceous conifer of related genera, especially a Douglas fir (Pseudotsuga) or a Scots pine (Pinus sylvestris)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fir"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Douglas fir","blocks":[{"text":"The Douglas fir is a conifer species in the pine family, Pinaceae. It is the tallest tree in the family. It is native to western North America and is also known as Douglas-fir, Douglas spruce, Oregon pine, and Columbian pine. There are three varieties: coast Douglas-fir, Rocky Mountain Douglas-fir and Mexican Douglas-fir."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Pseudotsuga_menziesii_4584.JPG/330px-Pseudotsuga_menziesii_4584.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Douglas_fir"},{"title":"Fir","blocks":[{"text":"Firs are coniferous trees belonging to the genus Abies in the family Pinaceae. There are approximately 48–65 extant species, found on mountains throughout much of North and Central America, Eurasia, and North Africa. The genus is most closely related to Keteleeria, a small genus confined to eastern Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Abies_koreana_%28szyszki%29.JPG/330px-Abies_koreana_%28szyszki%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fir"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Pseudotsuga_menziesii_MHNT.BOT.52.4.jpg/500px-Pseudotsuga_menziesii_MHNT.BOT.52.4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Pseudotsuga_menziesii_MHNT.BOT.52.4.jpg/1280px-Pseudotsuga_menziesii_MHNT.BOT.52.4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co